-- title:
--   Positivity of the apex-shrinking distance constant
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$ satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. Let $\delta = \delta(d, \vartheta)$ be the constant of Lemma 5.9 — the threshold ratio of distance to cube side beyond which passing from a cone of apex angle $\vartheta/2$ to one of apex angle $\vartheta$ absorbs an entire cube at each end. Then
--
--   $$\delta(d,\vartheta) > 0 .$$
--
--   Positivity is what makes the constant usable as a genuine threshold: the renormalization scheme chooses a scale step $\Delta$ larger than $\delta$, and the later constructions divide by $\delta$ or compare distances to $\delta$ times a cube side, both of which require $\delta \neq 0$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The exact value of the constant is not part of this statement; only its positivity is asserted, and it is asserted for every dimension $d$, including the degenerate $d = 0$. The hypotheses $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$ are exactly the range of apex angles the paper allows, and the upper bound keeps the half-angle in the range where the sine is positive: the constant is $\delta(d,\vartheta) = (\sqrt d + 1)/\sin(\vartheta/2)$, so it is $\sin(\vartheta/2)$, not $\sin\vartheta$, that has to stay positive. (The formalisation does not use the paper's value $3\sqrt d/(2\sin\vartheta)$, which is too small.)
